find all assignments (e,f,g,h) that satisfy all constraints (c1..c10). Then output them as list of tuples (e,f,g,h) sorted lexicographically (presumably ascending by e then f then g then h). Then we need to fill in the envelope with those tuples in payload.claim.value.tuples.\n\nWe must not produce any prose, only raw JSON matching the template. So we need to produce a JSON envelope with \"PROPOSE\" etc, with the correct tuples.\n\nWe need to compute all satisfying assignments for e,f,g,h given constraints. Let's parse constraints.\n\nConstraints:\n\nc1: variables a,b. Allowed pairs: [2,2], [2,1], [1,1], [3,1], [1,0]. So allowed (a,b) ∈ {(2,2),(2,1),(1,1),(3,1),(1,0)}.\n\nc2: variables b,c. Allowed pairs: [2,0], [3,0], [0,2], [3,1], [3,3].\n\nc3: variables c,d. Allowed pairs: [0,3], [2,0], [0,2], [2,3], [3,2].\n\nc4: variables d,a. Allowed pairs: [3,2], [3,1], [2,2], [3,3], [2,1].\n\nc5: variables e,f. Allowed pairs: [0,2], [2,2], [1,1], [0,1], [3,0].\n\nc6: variables f,g. Allowed pairs: [2,2], [0,2], [1,0], [0,0], [2,1].\n\nc7: variables g,h. Allowed pairs: [2,3], [0,1], [0,2], [0,0], [1,2].\n\nc8: variables h,e. Allowed pairs: [3,0], [1,1], [1,2], [2,3], [3,1].\n\nc9: variables b,e. Allowed pairs: [2,0], [1,2].\n\nc10: variables d,g. Allowed pairs: [3,2], [0,0].\n\nGoal: find all assignments for (e,f,g,h) that can be extended to values for a,b,c,d satisfying all constraints.\n\nThus we need to find all possible combos of e,f,g,h such that there exist a,b,c,d ∈ {0,1,2,3} meeting all constraints.\n\nThus it's a CSP with 8 variables; we need to enumerate all solutions for e,f,g,h.\n\nWe need to find all satisfying assignments for right component, i.e., all possible e,f,g,h values that can be part of a full solution.\n\nWe need to output those assignments (tuples) sorted lexicographically.\n\nWe need to find all solutions.\n\nWe could brute force by reasoning manually. Let's try to solve systematically.\n\nApproach: Use constraints to restrict possible values for a,b,c,d given e,f,g,h.\n\nWe have constraints linking left variables (a,b,c,d) and right variables (e,f,g,h). The constraints c5..c8 directly involve right variables. c9 and c10 also involve cross variables: c9: b,e; c10: d,g.\n\nThus we need to consider all possibilities for e,f,g,h that satisfy c5,c6,c7,c8,c9,c10 with some a,b,c,d that also satisfy c1..c4,c9,c10.\n\nThus we need to find all possible e,f,g,h for which there exist a,b,c,d satisfying all constraints.\n\nWe could attempt to enumerate all possibilities for e,f,g,h (4^4 = 256 combos). For each, test constraints c5..c8 (which only involve e,f,g,h). Then check if there exist a,b,c,d satisfying constraints c1..c4 and c9,c10 with those e,f,g,h.\n\nThus we need to find all e,f,g,h combos that satisfy:\n\n- (e,f) in allowed pairs for c5.\n- (f,g) in allowed pairs for c6.\n- (g,h) in allowed pairs for c7.\n- (h,e) in allowed pairs for c8.\n- (b,e) in allowed pairs for c9 (so b must be such that (b,e) is allowed).\n- (d,g) in allowed pairs for c10 (so d must be such that (d,g) is allowed).\n\nAdditionally, there must exist a,b,c,d such that:\n\n- (a,b) in allowed pairs for c1.\n- (b,c) in allowed pairs for c2.\n- (c,d) in allowed pairs for c3.\n- (d,a) in allowed pairs for c4.\n\nThus we need to find all e,f,g,h combos that satisfy the right side constraints and also allow existence of a,b,c,d satisfying left constraints with cross constraints.\n\nWe need to enumerate all possible combos. Let's start by enumerating possible (e,f,g,h) combos that satisfy c5,c6,c7,c8. Then for each, see if we can find a,b,c,d.\n\nFirst, list allowed pairs for each constraint:\n\nc5 (e,f): allowed = {(0,2), (2,2), (1,1), (0,1), (3,0)}.\n\nc6 (f,g): allowed = {(2,2), (0,2), (1,0), (0,0), (2,1)}.\n\nc7 (g,h): allowed = {(2,3), (0,1), (0,2), (0,0), (1,2)}.\n\nc8 (h,e): allowed = {(3,0), (1,1), (1,2), (2,3), (3,1)}.\n\nThus (e,f) must be one of those 5 combos; (f,g) must be one of those 5 combos; (g,h) one of those 5 combos; (h,e) one of those 5 combos.\n\nThus we need to find cycles of length 4 consistent: e->f->g->h->e.\n\nWe can treat this as a directed graph where each variable has domain values and edges are allowed pairs. We need to find all 4-tuples (e,f,g,h) such that each adjacent pair is allowed.\n\nWe can brute force manually by exploring possibilities.\n\nWe need to consider all possible values for each variable: 0,1,2,3.\n\nLet's list for each variable the possible values based on constraints:\n\n- e: appears in c5 (e,f) and c8 (h,e). So e must be such that there exists f with (e,f) in c5 and there exists h with (h,e) in c8. So e ∈ domain of c5 first component: values that appear as first element in allowed pairs for c5: from c5: e can be 0 (pair (0,2) and (0,1)), 2 (pair (2,2)), 1 (pair (1,1)), 3 (pair (3,0)). So e ∈ {0,1,2,3} all possible? Yes all four appear: 0,2,1,3. So e can be any domain.\n\n- f: appears in c5 (e,f) and c6 (f,g). f can be second component of c5 pairs: values: 2 (0,2), 2 (2,2), 1 (1,1), 1 (0,1), 0 (3,0). So f ∈ {0,1,2}. So f cannot be 3.\n\n- g: appears in c6 (f,g) and c7 (g,h). g can be second component of c6: values: 2 (2,2), 2 (0,2), 0 (1,0), 0 (0,0), 1 (2,1). So g ∈ {0,1,2}. So g cannot be 3.\n\n- h: appears in c7 (g,h) and c8 (h,e). h can be second component of c7: values: 3 (2,3), 1 (0,1), 2 (0,2), 0 (0,0), 2 (1,2). So h ∈ {0,1,2,3}? It appears 0,1,2,3. So all domain.\n\nThus possible values: e∈{0,1,2,3}, f∈{0,1,2}, g∈{0,1,2}, h∈{0,1,2,3}.\n\nNow we need to find all combos satisfying all four constraints.\n\nWe can treat this as a CSP of 4 variables with constraints as above. Let's enumerate all possibilities systematically.\n\nWe can start by enumerating all possible (e,f) pairs from c5 (5 combos). For each, enumerate all possible (f,g) pairs from c6 that match f. Then for each, enumerate (g,h) from c7 that match g. Then check (h,e) from c8.\n\nThus we need to find all cycles.\n\nLet's list c5 pairs:\n\nc5:\n1. (e=0, f=2)\n2. (e=2, f=2)\n3. (e=1, f=1)\n4. (e=0, f=1)\n5. (e=3, f=0)\n\nThus possible (e,f) combos.\n\nNow for each (e,f), find possible (f,g) from c6.\n\nc6 allowed pairs (f,g):\nA. (2,2)\nB. (0,2)\nC. (1,0)\nD. (0,0)\nE. (2,1)\n\nThus for each f value, possible g values:\n\n- If f=2: possible g = 2 (A) or 1 (E). So g ∈ {2,1}.\n\n- If f=0: possible g = 2 (B) or 0 (D). So g ∈ {2,0}.\n\n- If f=1: possible g = 0 (C). So g = 0.\n\nThus we can combine.\n\nNow for each (e,f,g) we need (g,h) from c7.\n\nc7 allowed pairs (g,h):\n1. (2,3)\n2. (0,1)\n3. (0,2)\n4. (0,0)\n5. (1,2)\n\nThus for each g value:\n\n- If g=2: possible h = 3 (pair 1). Also maybe others? g=2 appears only in pair (2,3). So h must be 3.\n\n- If g=0: possible h = 1 (pair 2), 2 (pair 3), 0 (pair 4). So h ∈ {0,1,2}.\n\n- If g=1: possible h = 2 (pair 5). So h = 2.\n\nThus for each g we have h possibilities.\n\nNow final constraint: (h,e) must be in c8.\n\nc8 allowed pairs (h,e):\n1. (3,0)\n2. (1,1)\n3. (1,2)\n4. (2,3)\n5. (3,1)\n\nThus for each (h,e) we need to check.\n\nThus we can enumerate all possibilities.\n\nLet's enumerate all combos step by step.\n\nWe have 5 possible (e,f) from c5. For each, we have possible g values based on f. Then h possibilities based on g. Then check (h,e) in c8.\n\nLet's create a systematic enumeration.\n\nCase 1: (e=0, f=2). f=2 => possible g ∈ {2,1}.\n\n- Subcase 1a: g=2. Then h must be 3 (since g=2 only yields h=3). So h=3. Check (h,e) = (3,0). Is (3,0) allowed in c8? Yes, c8 includes (3,0) as first pair. So (3,0) is allowed. So this yields a valid tuple (e=0,f=2,g=2,h=3). Check also other constraints? Already satisfied all. So (0,2,2,3) is a solution.\n\n- Subcase 1b: g=1. Then h must be 2 (since g=1 yields h=2). So h=2. Check (h,e) = (2,0). Is (2,0) allowed in c8? c8 allowed pairs: (3,0),(1,1),(1,2),(2,3),(3,1). (2,0) is not present. So invalid. So subcase 1b invalid.\n\nThus from case 1 we have only (0,2,2,3).\n\nCase 2: (e=2, f=2). f=2 => possible g ∈ {2,1}.\n\n- Subcase 2a: g=2 => h=3. Check (h,e) = (3,2). Is (3,2) allowed in c8? c8 allowed pairs: (3,0),(1,1),(1,2),(2,3),(3,1). (3,2) not present. So invalid.\n\n- Subcase 2b: g=1 => h=2. Check (h,e) = (2,2). Is (2,2) allowed? c8 does not have (2,2). So invalid.\n\nThus case 2 yields no solutions.\n\nCase 3: (e=1, f=1). f=1 => possible g = 0 (only). So g=0.\n\nNow g=0 => h ∈ {0,1,2} (three possibilities). For each h, check (h,e) = (h,1) must be in c8.\n\nc8 allowed pairs with e=1: (3,0) has e=0, not; (1,1) has h=1, e=1 -> allowed. (1,2) has h=1, e=2 -> not. (2,3) has e=3 -> not. (3,1) has h=3, e=1 -> allowed.\n\nThus allowed (h,e) pairs for e=1 are: (1,1) and (3,1). So h must be 1 or 3.\n\nBut h must be one of {0,1,2} from g=0 possibilities. So h can be 1 (allowed) or 3 (not allowed because h=3 not in {0,1,2}). So only h=1 works.\n\nThus subcase: (e=1,f=1,g=0,h=1) is valid.\n\nCheck: (g=0,h=1) is allowed? c7 includes (0,1) yes. So valid.\n\nThus case 3 yields (1,1,0,1).\n\nCase 4: (e=0, f=1). f=1 => possible g = 0 (only). So g=0.\n\nNow g=0 => h ∈ {0,1,2}. Check (h,e) = (h,0) must be allowed in c8.\n\nc8 allowed pairs with e=0: (3,0) has h=3, e=0 -> allowed. No other pair has e=0. So only (h=3) is allowed. But h must be from {0,1,2}. So no solution.\n\nThus case 4 yields none.\n\nCase 5: (e=3, f=0). f=0 => possible g ∈ {2,0}.\n\n- Subcase 5a: g=2 => h=3. Check (h,e) = (3,3). Is (3,3) allowed in c8? c8 allowed pairs: (3,0),(1,1),(1,2),(2,3),(3,1). (3,3) not present. So invalid.\n\n- Subcase 5b: g=0 => h ∈ {0,1,2}. Check (h,e) = (h,3) must be allowed.\n\nc8 allowed pairs with e=3: (2,3) has h=2, e=3 -> allowed. (3,1) has e=1, not. (3,0) e=0. (1,1) e=1. (1,2) e=2. So only (2,3) is allowed for e=3. So h must be 2.\n\nThus for g=0, h must be 2 (since h ∈ {0,1,2} and only 2 allowed). So h=2.\n\nCheck if (g=0,h=2) is allowed in c7: c7 includes (0,2) yes. So valid.\n\nThus case 5 yields (e=3,f=0,g=0,h=2).\n\nThus we have three candidate tuples from the direct constraints:\n\n- (0,2,2,3)\n- (1,1,0,1)\n- (3,0,0,2)\n\nBut we also need to check cross constraints c9 (b,e) and c10 (d,g) and existence of a,b,c,d satisfying left constraints.\n\nThus we need to see if for each of these tuples, there exist values for a,b,c,d satisfying left constraints and cross constraints.\n\nThus we need to solve left side variables a,b,c,d given constraints c1-c4 and cross constraints:\n\n- c9: (b,e) must be in allowed pairs: allowed pairs for c9 are [(2,0), (1,2)]. So (b,e) must be either (2,0) or (1,2). So for each e value, b must be determined accordingly:\n\nIf e=0: allowed (b,e) = (2,0) => b=2. Also (1,2) not applicable because e=2. So for e=0, b must be 2.\n\nIf e=2: allowed (b,e) = (1,2) => b=1. Also (2,0) not applicable because e=0. So for e=2, b must be 1.\n\nIf e=1: no allowed pair because e=1 not present. So any tuple with e=1 is impossible because c9 cannot be satisfied. Indeed c9 allowed pairs are (2,0) and (1,2). So e must be 0 or 2. So any tuple with e=1 is invalid. That eliminates (1,1,0,1) because e=1. So that tuple is not allowed.\n\nIf e=3: no allowed pair because e=3 not present. So any tuple with e=3 is invalid. That eliminates (3,0,0,2) because e=3. So only (0,2,2,3) has e=0, which is allowed: b must be 2.\n\nThus only (e=0,f=2,g=2,h=3) passes c9.\n\nThus we have only one candidate tuple for right side that satisfies c5-c9 constraints: (0,2,2,3). Let's double-check: e=0 => b=2. Good.\n\nNow we need to check c10: (d,g) must be in allowed pairs: allowed pairs for c10 are [(3,2), (0,0)]. So (d,g) must be either (3,2) or (0,0). Since g=2 in this tuple, we need d=3 (since (3,2) matches). So d must be 3.\n\nThus d=3.\n\nNow we have left variables partially determined: b=2, d=3, and we have cross constraints linking a,b,c,d via c1-c4.\n\nWe need to find a and c values such that:\n\n- c1: (a,b) allowed: (a,2) must be in allowed pairs for c1: allowed pairs for c1 are [(2,2),(2,1),(1,1),(3,1),(1,0)]. So we need (a,b) = (a,2) to be one of these. The second component is 2. Among allowed pairs, which have second component 2? (2,2) only. So a must be 2.\n\nThus a=2.\n\n- c4: (d,a) allowed: (3,2) must be in allowed pairs for c4: allowed pairs for c4 are [(3,2),(3,1),(2,2),(3,3),(2,1)]. (3,2) is indeed allowed. So d=3,a=2 satisfies c4.\n\n- c2: (b,c) allowed: (2,c) must be in allowed pairs for c2: allowed pairs for c2 are [(2,0),(3,0),(0,2),(3,1),(3,3)]. So we need (2,c) to be one of these. The first component 2 appears only in (2,0). So c must be 0.\n\nThus c=0.\n\n- c3: (c,d) allowed: (0,3) must be in allowed pairs for c3: allowed pairs for c3 are [(0,3),(2,0),(0,2),(2,3),(3,2)]. (0,3) is indeed allowed. So c=0,d=3 satisfies c3.\n\nThus all left constraints satisfied with a=2,b=2,c=0,d=3.\n\nThus the full assignment:\n\na=2,b=2,c=0,d=3,e=0,f=2,g=2,h=3.\n\nCheck all constraints:\n\nc1 (a,b) = (2,2) allowed. Yes.\n\nc2 (b,c) = (2,0) allowed. Yes.\n\nc3 (c,d) = (0,3) allowed. Yes.\n\nc4 (d,a) = (3,2) allowed. Yes.\n\nc5 (e,f) = (0,2) allowed. Yes.\n\nc6 (f,g) = (2,2) allowed. Yes.\n\nc7 (g,h) = (2,3) allowed. Yes.\n\nc8 (h,e) = (3,0) allowed. Yes.\n\nc9 (b,e) = (2,0) allowed. Yes.\n\nc10 (d,g) = (3,2) allowed. Yes.\n\nThus (e,f,g,h) = (0,2,2,3) is a valid solution.\n\nNow we need to enumerate all satisfying assignments for component right. So far we found exactly one solution. But we need to be careful: Did we miss any possibilities? Let's double-check the enumeration for (e,f,g,h) combos. We considered all possible (e,f) from c5 (5 combos). For each we enumerated possible (f,g) combos based on f. However, we might have missed some possibilities for f,g combos because we assumed only those f values, but there could be multiple g for given f, which we accounted. Also we considered all h possibilities for each g. So enumeration seems complete.\n\nBut we must double-check that there are no other possible (e,f) combos beyond those listed? c5 allowed pairs are exactly those five. So correct.\n\nThus the only possible (e,f,g,h) combos that satisfy c5-c8 are the three we found: (0,2,2,3), (1,1,0,1), (3,0,0,2). Let's double-check (1,1,0,1) and (3,0,0,2) satisfy c5-c8.\n\nCheck (1,1,0,1):\n\nc5: (e,f) = (1,1) allowed? Yes (1,1) is in c5.\n\nc6: (f,g) = (1,0) allowed? c6 includes (1,0) as (1,0) from pair C. Yes.\n\nc7: (g,h) = (0,1) allowed? c7 includes (0,1) as pair 2. Yes.\n\nc8: (h,e) = (1,1) allowed? c8 includes (1,1) as pair 2. Yes.\n\nThus (1,1,0,1) satisfies c5-c8.\n\nCheck (3,0,0,2):\n\nc5: (3,0) allowed? Yes (3,0) is in c5.\n\nc6: (0,0) allowed? c6 includes (0,0) as pair D. Yes.\n\nc7: (0,2) allowed? c7 includes (0,2) as pair 3. Yes.\n\nc8: (2,3) allowed? c8 includes (2,3) as pair 4. Yes.\n\nThus both satisfy c5-c8.\n\nThus we have three candidate right side assignments.\n\nNow we need to check cross constraints c9 and c10 for each.\n\nc9: (b,e) must be in allowed pairs [(2,0),(1,2)].\n\nThus for each e value:\n\n- For (0,2,2,3): e=0 => b must be 2 (allowed). So b=2.\n\n- For (1,1,0,1): e=1 => no allowed pair for e=1, thus impossible. So this tuple cannot be part of any solution.\n\n- For (3,0,0,2): e=3 => no allowed pair for e=3, thus impossible. So this tuple cannot be part of any solution.\n\nThus only (0,2,2,3) passes c9.\n\nNow c10: (d,g) must be in allowed pairs [(3,2),(0,0)].\n\n- For (0,2,2,3): g=2 => d must be 3 (since (3,2) allowed). So d=3.\n\n- For the other two, they are already invalid due to c9, so no need.\n\nThus only one right side assignment yields a consistent left side.\n\nNow we need to ensure that left side constraints c1-c4 can be satisfied with the required b and d values.\n\nWe found that for (0,2,2,3) we can set a=2,b=2,c=0,d=3. That satisfies all.\n\nThus the only satisfying assignment for component right is (0,2,2,3). However, we need to verify if there could be alternative assignments for a,b,c,d that also satisfy constraints for the same (e,f,g,h) tuple. But that doesn't affect right side enumeration; we only need to list right side tuples. So we have exactly one tuple.\n\nThus the answer is a list containing one tuple: [0,2,2,3].\n\nNow we need to output the envelope with payload.claim.value.tuples containing that tuple.\n\nThe envelope template includes many fields. We need to edit only payload.claim.value.tuples. So we need to replace the placeholder tuple(s) with the correct tuple(s). The placeholder currently has a single tuple [0,0,0,0]. We need to replace with [0,2,2,3] as the only tuple.\n\nThus final JSON envelope should have \"tuples\": [[0,2,2,3]].\n\nWe need to output exactly one JSON envelope matching the supplied template; no prose outside JSON; no markdown; raw unformatted JSON starting with { and ending with }.\n\nThus we need to output something like:\n\n{\n  \"intent\": \"PROPOSE\",\n  \"message_id\": \"csp-5110-right-proposer-1\",\n  \"payload\": {\n    \"claim\": {\n      \"claim_id\": \"right-tuples\",\n      \"dependencies\": [\n        {\n          \"claim_revision\": 1,\n          \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-5110\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"provenance\": {\n        \"message_id\": \"csp-5110-right-proposer-1\"\n      },\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1,\n      \"status\": \"active\",\n      \"value\": {\n        \"component\": \"right\",\n        \"tuples\": [\n          [\n            0,\n            2,\n            2,\n            3\n          ]\n        ],\n        \"variables\": [\n          \"e\",\n          \"f\",\n          \"g\",\n          \"h\"\n        ]\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"problem_id\": \"csp-5110\",\n  \"read_refs\": [\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-5110\",\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1\n    },\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"claim:right-tuples\",\n      \"revision\": 0,\n      \"state_revision\": 0\n    }\n  ],\n  \"recipients\": [\n    \"left-reviewer\",\n    \"shared-integrator\"\n  ],\n  \"reply_to\": null,\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"sender\": \"right-proposer\"\n}\n\nWe need to ensure that JSON formatting is correct (no trailing commas). Also ensure that numbers are JSON integers (they are). Also ensure that the envelope's fields are exactly as in template, except the tuples.\n\nThe template includes \"read_refs\": includes a reference to claim:right-tuples revision 0. That's fine.\n\nThus final answer is that JSON.\n\nBut before finalizing, we must double-check that there are no other possible right side assignments that we missed due to potential multiple possibilities for left side variables that could allow other right side assignments via c9 and c10. Let's double-check thoroughly.\n\nWe enumerated all possible (e,f,g,h) combos that satisfy c5-c8. There were three. Two were eliminated by c9 because e not in {0,2}. However, c9 also imposes that b must be a specific value based on e. Could there be alternative assignments for b that satisfy c9 for e=0 or e=2? For e=0, allowed (b,e) pairs are (2,0) only. So b must be 2. For e=2, allowed (b,e) pairs are (1,2) only. So b must be 1. So for e=2, b=1. So if there were any (e,f,g,h) tuple with e=2, we would need b=1. Did we have any tuple with e=2? Let's check the three candidate tuples:\n\n- (0,2,2,3): e=0.\n- (1,1,0,1): e=1.\n- (3,0,0,2): e=3.\n\nThus none have e=2. So no other possibilities.\n\nBut could there be other (e,f,g,h) combos that satisfy c5-c8 but we missed due to multiple possible g values for a given f? Let's double-check enumeration thoroughly.\n\nWe enumerated all (e,f) from c5: 5 combos.\n\nFor each (e,f), we considered possible g based on f. However, we need to ensure we considered all possible g for each f. Let's list c6 pairs again:\n\nc6 allowed pairs: (2,2), (0,2), (1,0), (0,0), (2,1). So mapping f->g:\n\n- f=2: g ∈ {2,1}\n- f=0: g ∈ {2,0}\n- f=1: g ∈ {0}\n- f=3: g ∈ {}? No f=3 does not appear as first component in any c6 pair. So any tuple with f=3 is impossible. Indeed c5 includes (e=3,f=0) only; no (e,3) combos. So f cannot be 3. So f ∈ {0,1,2}. Good.\n\nThus our enumeration considered f values from c5: f can be 2 (cases 1 and 2), f=1 (case 3), f=0 (case 4 and 5). So we covered all.\n\nNow for each (e,f) we considered all possible g values based on f.\n\nCase 1: (e=0,f=2). f=2 => g ∈ {2,1}. We considered both.\n\nCase 2: (e=2,f=2). f=2 => g ∈ {2,1}. We considered both.\n\nCase 3: (e=1,f=1). f=1 => g=0 only. We considered.\n\nCase 4: (e=0,f=1). f=1 => g=0 only. We considered.\n\nCase 5: (e=3,f=0). f=0 => g ∈ {2,0}. We considered both.\n\nThus we enumerated all possibilities.\n\nNow for each g we considered all possible h based on g.\n\nc7 mapping g->h:\n\n- g=2: h=3 only.\n- g=1: h=2 only.\n- g=0: h ∈ {0,1,2}.\n\nThus for each g we considered all possibilities.\n\nNow for each (h,e) we checked c8.\n\nThus enumeration is complete.\n\nThus only three possible (e,f,g,h) combos satisfy c5-c8.\n\nThus only one passes c9 and c10.\n\nThus answer is correct.\n\nNow we need to ensure that the envelope fields are correct. The template includes \"read_refs\" with a reference to claim:right-tuples revision 0. That seems to be a reference to a previous claim. We can keep as is.\n\nWe need to ensure that the JSON is valid: no trailing commas. Also ensure that the \"tuples\" array contains exactly one tuple with four integers.\n\nThus final output.\n\nOne nuance: The envelope includes \"payload.claim.value.tuples\": [ [0,0,0,0] ] originally. We need to replace with [ [0,2,2,3] ].\n\nThus final JSON.\n\nNow we need to output exactly that JSON, no extra whitespace? It can have whitespace, but it's fine. The instruction says \"Output raw unformatted JSON starting with { and ending with }.\" It doesn't forbid whitespace, but \"unformatted\" might mean no extra formatting like code fences. But whitespace inside JSON is fine. We'll output pretty printed JSON (like above). That should be fine.\n\nThus answer.\n\nBut we must double-check that the \"message_id\" matches the template. It is \"csp-5110-right-proposer-1\". That's fine.\n\nNow we should also consider if there might be multiple solutions for right side if we consider alternative assignments for left variables that could allow other right side assignments. However, we already considered all possible right side assignments that satisfy c5-c8. Those are the only ones. So no other right side assignments exist.\n\nThus answer is correct.\n\nNow we need to output JSON. Let's produce it.","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The problem: We have a CSP with variables a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h each domain {0,1,2,3}. Constraints c1..c10 given. The role task: \"Enumerate ALL and ONLY satisfying assignments for component right, sorted lexicographically. Edit only payload.claim.value.tuples in the envelope. Each row contains exactly four domain integers in the listed variable order.\"\n\nComponent right is variables e,f,g,h. So we need to find all assignments (e,f,g,h) that satisfy all constraints (c1..c10). Then output them as list of tuples (e,f,g,h) sorted lexicographically (presumably ascending by e then f then g then h). Then we need to fill in the envelope with those tuples in payload.claim.value.tuples.\n\nWe must not produce any prose, only raw JSON matching the template. So we need to produce a JSON envelope with \"PROPOSE\" etc, with the correct tuples.\n\nWe need to compute all satisfying assignments for e,f,g,h given constraints. Let's parse constraints.\n\nConstraints:\n\nc1: variables a,b. Allowed pairs: [2,2], [2,1], [1,1], [3,1], [1,0]. So allowed (a,b) ∈ {(2,2),(2,1),(1,1),(3,1),(1,0)}.\n\nc2: variables b,c. Allowed pairs: [2,0], [3,0], [0,2], [3,1], [3,3].\n\nc3: variables c,d. Allowed pairs: [0,3], [2,0], [0,2], [2,3], [3,2].\n\nc4: variables d,a. Allowed pairs: [3,2], [3,1], [2,2], [3,3], [2,1].\n\nc5: variables e,f. Allowed pairs: [0,2], [2,2], [1,1], [0,1], [3,0].\n\nc6: variables f,g. Allowed pairs: [2,2], [0,2], [1,0], [0,0], [2,1].\n\nc7: variables g,h. Allowed pairs: [2,3], [0,1], [0,2], [0,0], [1,2].\n\nc8: variables h,e. Allowed pairs: [3,0], [1,1], [1,2], [2,3], [3,1].\n\nc9: variables b,e. Allowed pairs: [2,0], [1,2].\n\nc10: variables d,g. Allowed pairs: [3,2], [0,0].\n\nGoal: find all assignments for (e,f,g,h) that can be extended to values for a,b,c,d satisfying all constraints.\n\nThus we need to find all possible combos of e,f,g,h such that there exist a,b,c,d ∈ {0,1,2,3} meeting all constraints.\n\nThus it's a CSP with 8 variables; we need to enumerate all solutions for e,f,g,h.\n\nWe need to find all satisfying assignments for right component, i.e., all possible e,f,g,h values that can be part of a full solution.\n\nWe need to output those assignments (tuples) sorted lexicographically.\n\nWe need to find all solutions.\n\nWe could brute force by reasoning manually. Let's try to solve systematically.\n\nApproach: Use constraints to restrict possible values for a,b,c,d given e,f,g,h.\n\nWe have constraints linking left variables (a,b,c,d) and right variables (e,f,g,h). The constraints c5..c8 directly involve right variables. c9 and c10 also involve cross variables: c9: b,e; c10: d,g.\n\nThus we need to consider all possibilities for e,f,g,h that satisfy c5,c6,c7,c8,c9,c10 with some a,b,c,d that also satisfy c1..c4,c9,c10.\n\nThus we need to find all possible e,f,g,h for which there exist a,b,c,d satisfying all constraints.\n\nWe could attempt to enumerate all possibilities for e,f,g,h (4^4 = 256 combos). For each, test constraints c5..c8 (which only involve e,f,g,h). Then check if there exist a,b,c,d satisfying constraints c1..c4 and c9,c10 with those e,f,g,h.\n\nThus we need to find all e,f,g,h combos that satisfy:\n\n- (e,f) in allowed pairs for c5.\n- (f,g) in allowed pairs for c6.\n- (g,h) in allowed pairs for c7.\n- (h,e) in allowed pairs for c8.\n- (b,e) in allowed pairs for c9 (so b must be such that (b,e) is allowed).\n- (d,g) in allowed pairs for c10 (so d must be such that (d,g) is allowed).\n\nAdditionally, there must exist a,b,c,d such that:\n\n- (a,b) in allowed pairs for c1.\n- (b,c) in allowed pairs for c2.\n- (c,d) in allowed pairs for c3.\n- (d,a) in allowed pairs for c4.\n\nThus we need to find all e,f,g,h combos that satisfy the right side constraints and also allow existence of a,b,c,d satisfying left constraints with cross constraints.\n\nWe need to enumerate all possible combos. Let's start by enumerating possible (e,f,g,h) combos that satisfy c5,c6,c7,c8. Then for each, see if we can find a,b,c,d.\n\nFirst, list allowed pairs for each constraint:\n\nc5 (e,f): allowed = {(0,2), (2,2), (1,1), (0,1), (3,0)}.\n\nc6 (f,g): allowed = {(2,2), (0,2), (1,0), (0,0), (2,1)}.\n\nc7 (g,h): allowed = {(2,3), (0,1), (0,2), (0,0), (1,2)}.\n\nc8 (h,e): allowed = {(3,0), (1,1), (1,2), (2,3), (3,1)}.\n\nThus (e,f) must be one of those 5 combos; (f,g) must be one of those 5 combos; (g,h) one of those 5 combos; (h,e) one of those 5 combos.\n\nThus we need to find cycles of length 4 consistent: e->f->g->h->e.\n\nWe can treat this as a directed graph where each variable has domain values and edges are allowed pairs. We need to find all 4-tuples (e,f,g,h) such that each adjacent pair is allowed.\n\nWe can brute force manually by exploring possibilities.\n\nWe need to consider all possible values for each variable: 0,1,2,3.\n\nLet's list for each variable the possible values based on constraints:\n\n- e: appears in c5 (e,f) and c8 (h,e). So e must be such that there exists f with (e,f) in c5 and there exists h with (h,e) in c8. So e ∈ domain of c5 first component: values that appear as first element in allowed pairs for c5: from c5: e can be 0 (pair (0,2) and (0,1)), 2 (pair (2,2)), 1 (pair (1,1)), 3 (pair (3,0)). So e ∈ {0,1,2,3} all possible? Yes all four appear: 0,2,1,3. So e can be any domain.\n\n- f: appears in c5 (e,f) and c6 (f,g). f can be second component of c5 pairs: values: 2 (0,2), 2 (2,2), 1 (1,1), 1 (0,1), 0 (3,0). So f ∈ {0,1,2}. So f cannot be 3.\n\n- g: appears in c6 (f,g) and c7 (g,h). g can be second component of c6: values: 2 (2,2), 2 (0,2), 0 (1,0), 0 (0,0), 1 (2,1). So g ∈ {0,1,2}. So g cannot be 3.\n\n- h: appears in c7 (g,h) and c8 (h,e). h can be second component of c7: values: 3 (2,3), 1 (0,1), 2 (0,2), 0 (0,0), 2 (1,2). So h ∈ {0,1,2,3}? It appears 0,1,2,3. So all domain.\n\nThus possible values: e∈{0,1,2,3}, f∈{0,1,2}, g∈{0,1,2}, h∈{0,1,2,3}.\n\nNow we need to find all combos satisfying all four constraints.\n\nWe can treat this as a CSP of 4 variables with constraints as above. Let's enumerate all possibilities systematically.\n\nWe can start by enumerating all possible (e,f) pairs from c5 (5 combos). For each, enumerate all possible (f,g) pairs from c6 that match f. Then for each, enumerate (g,h) from c7 that match g. Then check (h,e) from c8.\n\nThus we need to find all cycles.\n\nLet's list c5 pairs:\n\nc5:\n1. (e=0, f=2)\n2. (e=2, f=2)\n3. (e=1, f=1)\n4. (e=0, f=1)\n5. (e=3, f=0)\n\nThus possible (e,f) combos.\n\nNow for each (e,f), find possible (f,g) from c6.\n\nc6 allowed pairs (f,g):\nA. (2,2)\nB. (0,2)\nC. (1,0)\nD. (0,0)\nE. (2,1)\n\nThus for each f value, possible g values:\n\n- If f=2: possible g = 2 (A) or 1 (E). So g ∈ {2,1}.\n\n- If f=0: possible g = 2 (B) or 0 (D). So g ∈ {2,0}.\n\n- If f=1: possible g = 0 (C). So g = 0.\n\nThus we can combine.\n\nNow for each (e,f,g) we need (g,h) from c7.\n\nc7 allowed pairs (g,h):\n1. (2,3)\n2. (0,1)\n3. (0,2)\n4. (0,0)\n5. (1,2)\n\nThus for each g value:\n\n- If g=2: possible h = 3 (pair 1). Also maybe others? g=2 appears only in pair (2,3). So h must be 3.\n\n- If g=0: possible h = 1 (pair 2), 2 (pair 3), 0 (pair 4). So h ∈ {0,1,2}.\n\n- If g=1: possible h = 2 (pair 5). So h = 2.\n\nThus for each g we have h possibilities.\n\nNow final constraint: (h,e) must be in c8.\n\nc8 allowed pairs (h,e):\n1. (3,0)\n2. (1,1)\n3. (1,2)\n4. (2,3)\n5. (3,1)\n\nThus for each (h,e) we need to check.\n\nThus we can enumerate all possibilities.\n\nLet's enumerate all combos step by step.\n\nWe have 5 possible (e,f) from c5. For each, we have possible g values based on f. Then h possibilities based on g. Then check (h,e) in c8.\n\nLet's create a systematic enumeration.\n\nCase 1: (e=0, f=2). f=2 => possible g ∈ {2,1}.\n\n- Subcase 1a: g=2. Then h must be 3 (since g=2 only yields h=3). So h=3. Check (h,e) = (3,0). Is (3,0) allowed in c8? Yes, c8 includes (3,0) as first pair. So (3,0) is allowed. So this yields a valid tuple (e=0,f=2,g=2,h=3). Check also other constraints? Already satisfied all. So (0,2,2,3) is a solution.\n\n- Subcase 1b: g=1. Then h must be 2 (since g=1 yields h=2). So h=2. Check (h,e) = (2,0). Is (2,0) allowed in c8? c8 allowed pairs: (3,0),(1,1),(1,2),(2,3),(3,1). (2,0) is not present. So invalid. So subcase 1b invalid.\n\nThus from case 1 we have only (0,2,2,3).\n\nCase 2: (e=2, f=2). f=2 => possible g ∈ {2,1}.\n\n- Subcase 2a: g=2 => h=3. Check (h,e) = (3,2). Is (3,2) allowed in c8? c8 allowed pairs: (3,0),(1,1),(1,2),(2,3),(3,1). (3,2) not present. So invalid.\n\n- Subcase 2b: g=1 => h=2. Check (h,e) = (2,2). Is (2,2) allowed? c8 does not have (2,2). So invalid.\n\nThus case 2 yields no solutions.\n\nCase 3: (e=1, f=1). f=1 => possible g = 0 (only). So g=0.\n\nNow g=0 => h ∈ {0,1,2} (three possibilities). For each h, check (h,e) = (h,1) must be in c8.\n\nc8 allowed pairs with e=1: (3,0) has e=0, not; (1,1) has h=1, e=1 -> allowed. (1,2) has h=1, e=2 -> not. (2,3) has e=3 -> not. (3,1) has h=3, e=1 -> allowed.\n\nThus allowed (h,e) pairs for e=1 are: (1,1) and (3,1). So h must be 1 or 3.\n\nBut h must be one of {0,1,2} from g=0 possibilities. So h can be 1 (allowed) or 3 (not allowed because h=3 not in {0,1,2}). So only h=1 works.\n\nThus subcase: (e=1,f=1,g=0,h=1) is valid.\n\nCheck: (g=0,h=1) is allowed? c7 includes (0,1) yes. So valid.\n\nThus case 3 yields (1,1,0,1).\n\nCase 4: (e=0, f=1). f=1 => possible g = 0 (only). So g=0.\n\nNow g=0 => h ∈ {0,1,2}. Check (h,e) = (h,0) must be allowed in c8.\n\nc8 allowed pairs with e=0: (3,0) has h=3, e=0 -> allowed. No other pair has e=0. So only (h=3) is allowed. But h must be from {0,1,2}. So no solution.\n\nThus case 4 yields none.\n\nCase 5: (e=3, f=0). f=0 => possible g ∈ {2,0}.\n\n- Subcase 5a: g=2 => h=3. Check (h,e) = (3,3). Is (3,3) allowed in c8? c8 allowed pairs: (3,0),(1,1),(1,2),(2,3),(3,1). (3,3) not present. So invalid.\n\n- Subcase 5b: g=0 => h ∈ {0,1,2}. Check (h,e) = (h,3) must be allowed.\n\nc8 allowed pairs with e=3: (2,3) has h=2, e=3 -> allowed. (3,1) has e=1, not. (3,0) e=0. (1,1) e=1. (1,2) e=2. So only (2,3) is allowed for e=3. So h must be 2.\n\nThus for g=0, h must be 2 (since h ∈ {0,1,2} and only 2 allowed). So h=2.\n\nCheck if (g=0,h=2) is allowed in c7: c7 includes (0,2) yes. So valid.\n\nThus case 5 yields (e=3,f=0,g=0,h=2).\n\nThus we have three candidate tuples from the direct constraints:\n\n- (0,2,2,3)\n- (1,1,0,1)\n- (3,0,0,2)\n\nBut we also need to check cross constraints c9 (b,e) and c10 (d,g) and existence of a,b,c,d satisfying left constraints.\n\nThus we need to see if for each of these tuples, there exist values for a,b,c,d satisfying left constraints and cross constraints.\n\nThus we need to solve left side variables a,b,c,d given constraints c1-c4 and cross constraints:\n\n- c9: (b,e) must be in allowed pairs: allowed pairs for c9 are [(2,0), (1,2)]. So (b,e) must be either (2,0) or (1,2). So for each e value, b must be determined accordingly:\n\nIf e=0: allowed (b,e) = (2,0) => b=2. Also (1,2) not applicable because e=2. So for e=0, b must be 2.\n\nIf e=2: allowed (b,e) = (1,2) => b=1. Also (2,0) not applicable because e=0. So for e=2, b must be 1.\n\nIf e=1: no allowed pair because e=1 not present. So any tuple with e=1 is impossible because c9 cannot be satisfied. Indeed c9 allowed pairs are (2,0) and (1,2). So e must be 0 or 2. So any tuple with e=1 is invalid. That eliminates (1,1,0,1) because e=1. So that tuple is not allowed.\n\nIf e=3: no allowed pair because e=3 not present. So any tuple with e=3 is invalid. That eliminates (3,0,0,2) because e=3. So only (0,2,2,3) has e=0, which is allowed: b must be 2.\n\nThus only (e=0,f=2,g=2,h=3) passes c9.\n\nThus we have only one candidate tuple for right side that satisfies c5-c9 constraints: (0,2,2,3). Let's double-check: e=0 => b=2. Good.\n\nNow we need to check c10: (d,g) must be in allowed pairs: allowed pairs for c10 are [(3,2), (0,0)]. So (d,g) must be either (3,2) or (0,0). Since g=2 in this tuple, we need d=3 (since (3,2) matches). So d must be 3.\n\nThus d=3.\n\nNow we have left variables partially determined: b=2, d=3, and we have cross constraints linking a,b,c,d via c1-c4.\n\nWe need to find a and c values such that:\n\n- c1: (a,b) allowed: (a,2) must be in allowed pairs for c1: allowed pairs for c1 are [(2,2),(2,1),(1,1),(3,1),(1,0)]. So we need (a,b) = (a,2) to be one of these. The second component is 2. Among allowed pairs, which have second component 2? (2,2) only. So a must be 2.\n\nThus a=2.\n\n- c4: (d,a) allowed: (3,2) must be in allowed pairs for c4: allowed pairs for c4 are [(3,2),(3,1),(2,2),(3,3),(2,1)]. (3,2) is indeed allowed. So d=3,a=2 satisfies c4.\n\n- c2: (b,c) allowed: (2,c) must be in allowed pairs for c2: allowed pairs for c2 are [(2,0),(3,0),(0,2),(3,1),(3,3)]. So we need (2,c) to be one of these. The first component 2 appears only in (2,0). So c must be 0.\n\nThus c=0.\n\n- c3: (c,d) allowed: (0,3) must be in allowed pairs for c3: allowed pairs for c3 are [(0,3),(2,0),(0,2),(2,3),(3,2)]. (0,3) is indeed allowed. So c=0,d=3 satisfies c3.\n\nThus all left constraints satisfied with a=2,b=2,c=0,d=3.\n\nThus the full assignment:\n\na=2,b=2,c=0,d=3,e=0,f=2,g=2,h=3.\n\nCheck all constraints:\n\nc1 (a,b) = (2,2) allowed. Yes.\n\nc2 (b,c) = (2,0) allowed. Yes.\n\nc3 (c,d) = (0,3) allowed. Yes.\n\nc4 (d,a) = (3,2) allowed. Yes.\n\nc5 (e,f) = (0,2) allowed. Yes.\n\nc6 (f,g) = (2,2) allowed. Yes.\n\nc7 (g,h) = (2,3) allowed. Yes.\n\nc8 (h,e) = (3,0) allowed. Yes.\n\nc9 (b,e) = (2,0) allowed. Yes.\n\nc10 (d,g) = (3,2) allowed. Yes.\n\nThus (e,f,g,h) = (0,2,2,3) is a valid solution.\n\nNow we need to enumerate all satisfying assignments for component right. So far we found exactly one solution. But we need to be careful: Did we miss any possibilities? Let's double-check the enumeration for (e,f,g,h) combos. We considered all possible (e,f) from c5 (5 combos). For each we enumerated possible (f,g) combos based on f. However, we might have missed some possibilities for f,g combos because we assumed only those f values, but there could be multiple g for given f, which we accounted. Also we considered all h possibilities for each g. So enumeration seems complete.\n\nBut we must double-check that there are no other possible (e,f) combos beyond those listed? c5 allowed pairs are exactly those five. So correct.\n\nThus the only possible (e,f,g,h) combos that satisfy c5-c8 are the three we found: (0,2,2,3), (1,1,0,1), (3,0,0,2). Let's double-check (1,1,0,1) and (3,0,0,2) satisfy c5-c8.\n\nCheck (1,1,0,1):\n\nc5: (e,f) = (1,1) allowed? Yes (1,1) is in c5.\n\nc6: (f,g) = (1,0) allowed? c6 includes (1,0) as (1,0) from pair C. Yes.\n\nc7: (g,h) = (0,1) allowed? c7 includes (0,1) as pair 2. Yes.\n\nc8: (h,e) = (1,1) allowed? c8 includes (1,1) as pair 2. Yes.\n\nThus (1,1,0,1) satisfies c5-c8.\n\nCheck (3,0,0,2):\n\nc5: (3,0) allowed? Yes (3,0) is in c5.\n\nc6: (0,0) allowed? c6 includes (0,0) as pair D. Yes.\n\nc7: (0,2) allowed? c7 includes (0,2) as pair 3. Yes.\n\nc8: (2,3) allowed? c8 includes (2,3) as pair 4. Yes.\n\nThus both satisfy c5-c8.\n\nThus we have three candidate right side assignments.\n\nNow we need to check cross constraints c9 and c10 for each.\n\nc9: (b,e) must be in allowed pairs [(2,0),(1,2)].\n\nThus for each e value:\n\n- For (0,2,2,3): e=0 => b must be 2 (allowed). So b=2.\n\n- For (1,1,0,1): e=1 => no allowed pair for e=1, thus impossible. So this tuple cannot be part of any solution.\n\n- For (3,0,0,2): e=3 => no allowed pair for e=3, thus impossible. So this tuple cannot be part of any solution.\n\nThus only (0,2,2,3) passes c9.\n\nNow c10: (d,g) must be in allowed pairs [(3,2),(0,0)].\n\n- For (0,2,2,3): g=2 => d must be 3 (since (3,2) allowed). So d=3.\n\n- For the other two, they are already invalid due to c9, so no need.\n\nThus only one right side assignment yields a consistent left side.\n\nNow we need to ensure that left side constraints c1-c4 can be satisfied with the required b and d values.\n\nWe found that for (0,2,2,3) we can set a=2,b=2,c=0,d=3. That satisfies all.\n\nThus the only satisfying assignment for component right is (0,2,2,3). However, we need to verify if there could be alternative assignments for a,b,c,d that also satisfy constraints for the same (e,f,g,h) tuple. But that doesn't affect right side enumeration; we only need to list right side tuples. So we have exactly one tuple.\n\nThus the answer is a list containing one tuple: [0,2,2,3].\n\nNow we need to output the envelope with payload.claim.value.tuples containing that tuple.\n\nThe envelope template includes many fields. We need to edit only payload.claim.value.tuples. So we need to replace the placeholder tuple(s) with the correct tuple(s). The placeholder currently has a single tuple [0,0,0,0]. We need to replace with [0,2,2,3] as the only tuple.\n\nThus final JSON envelope should have \"tuples\": [[0,2,2,3]].\n\nWe need to output exactly one JSON envelope matching the supplied template; no prose outside JSON; no markdown; raw unformatted JSON starting with { and ending with }.\n\nThus we need to output something like:\n\n{\n  \"intent\": \"PROPOSE\",\n  \"message_id\": \"csp-5110-right-proposer-1\",\n  \"payload\": {\n    \"claim\": {\n      \"claim_id\": \"right-tuples\",\n      \"dependencies\": [\n        {\n          \"claim_revision\": 1,\n          \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-5110\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"provenance\": {\n        \"message_id\": \"csp-5110-right-proposer-1\"\n      },\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1,\n      \"status\": \"active\",\n      \"value\": {\n        \"component\": \"right\",\n    